  "articleBody": "Getting new tile in your bathroom can totally transform the space, but there are some common mistakes that can turn excitement into frustration. Let's make sure you avoid them! First off, inadequate substrate preparation is a huge no-no. Tiling over a weak, uneven, or damaged surface is just asking for cracked tiles, ugly lippage (where tiles aren’t flush), or loose areas down the line. Always start with a solid, level base! Secondly, poor or incomplete waterproofing, especially in showers and around tubs, is a recipe for disaster. If water gets behind your tiles, you’re looking at leaks, mold, and hidden damage. Corners, niches, and plumbing penetrations are often overlooked spots, so make sure they're sealed perfectly. Another common issue is using the wrong adhesive or grout. Not all thinsets are created equal, especially for large-format tiles or natural stone. Using the wrong one can cause staining, warping, or bond failure. Also, watch out for incorrect slope in shower floors. If the water doesn't drain properly, you’ll end up with standing puddles. Lastly, skipping movement joints or using rigid caulk where flexible sealant is needed can cause grout to crack as your house naturally expands and contracts. Doing things right the first time prevents costly repairs. That’s why having experienced professionals is so important – we handle these details with care. Thinking about new tile? Give us a call!",
